"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"### Tested versions\n\n4.5-beta3 with Jolt\n\n### System information\n\nWindows 11 Forward+ nvidia 3070 Jolt Physics\n\n### Issue description\n\n## Summary  \nWhen a stack of RigidBody3D nodes enters sleep mode, sweeping another body, one configured with no collision layers or masks, through the stack unexpectedly wakes the entire stack, despite no collision layers in common.\n\n## Expected Behavior  \nA body with no collision layers and no collision mask (and with contact monitoring off) should not interact with or wake other physics bodies. The sleeping stack should remain asleep when such a “ghost” body passes through it.\n\n## Actual Behavior  \nDespite having zero layers/masks, the swept body still triggers a wake-up on the entire stack of RigidBody3D nodes, causing them to wobble and re-simulate physics.\n\n## Additional Information  \n- Possible cause: broadphase overlap tests or Jolt’s internal wake-up logic do not check collision layers/masks before waking sleeping bodies.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n1. Create a scene with 10 identical 1×1×1 boxes:  \n   - Each box is a RigidBody3D\n   - Attach a BoxCollider3D to each.  \n   - Position them in a perfect vertical stack (Z-axis separation = box height).  \n2. Start the scene and wait for the boxes to settle; they should wobble briefly then enter sleep mode.  \n3. Instance or spawn a new 1×1×1 box:  \n   - RigidBody3D set to “Sleeping” on start.  \n   - Collision → Layer: None, Mask: None.  \n   - Contact monitoring disabled.  \n   - Directly sweep/move this frozen box through the side of the sleeping stack using a script\n4. Observe the stack: instead of remaining at rest, all 10 boxes wake and begin wobbling again.\n\n### Minimal reproduction project (MRP)\n\nIt's easier/faster to create this in Godot than downloading a MRP.","closed_by":{"login":"Repiteo"